    "description": "A payment orchestration service calls an external card processor for every checkout. During last month's outage the processor answered with 2-second timeouts and 503s for 25 minutes; the orchestrator kept retrying hard, its own worker pool saturated, and the checkout flow for unrelated payment methods went down with it. Operations want the orchestrator to notice that the downstream dependency is unhealthy, stop sending traffic for a cooling-off period, let a small amount of probe traffic through to detect recovery, and otherwise fail fast with a clear error instead of queueing work that cannot succeed. The decision to implement: how to wrap calls to the unreliable dependency so repeated failures stop the traffic before the failure spreads to callers.",
    "decision_statement": "Wrap calls to the unreliable dependency so consecutive failures suspend traffic and a probe detects recovery.",
    "notes": "bulkhead/fail-fast are the overloading axes; retry-with-backoff is the near-miss sibling."
  },
